combase: Use one RPC endpoint per process, not one per apartment.

Each single-threaded COM apartment registered its own ncalrpc endpoint, \pipe\OLE_<pid><tid>,
which is never removed. Even an STA that only calls CoWaitForMultipleHandles registers one. rpcrt4's
listener waits on one handle per endpoint plus one, so at 64 endpoints the wait fails ("err:rpc:
rpcrt4_protseq_np_wait_for_new_connection wait failed with error 87"), the listener exits, and every
call between apartments fails from then on. PDF-XChange Editor's worker threads hit that within
minutes (they make new STAs every minute), then deadlock and crash. See WineHQ bug 46871.

Windows also uses a single endpoint per process. Calls find their apartment from the IPID, set as
the binding's object UUID, so the endpoint never mattered for that. The high 32 bits of an OXID are
the process ID (apartment.c), so a client can still derive a server's endpoint from its OXID.

Applied by scripts/wine/patch-combase.

--- a/dlls/combase/rpc.c
+++ b/dlls/combase/rpc.c
@@ -72,11 +72,14 @@
     RPC_SERVER_INTERFACE If; /* interface registered with the RPC runtime */
 };

-/* get the pipe endpoint specified of the specified apartment */
+/* get the pipe endpoint of the process that owns the specified apartment */
 static inline void get_rpc_endpoint(LPWSTR endpoint, const OXID *oxid)
 {
-    /* FIXME: should get endpoint from rpcss */
-    wsprintfW(endpoint, L"\\pipe\\OLE_%016I64x", *oxid);
+    /* One endpoint per process, as on Windows: calls find their apartment from the IPID, not the
+     * endpoint. One per apartment (the OXID's low 32 bits are the thread ID) adds an endpoint for
+     * each STA, never removed, and rpcrt4's listener fails once there are 64: its wait then has
+     * more than MAXIMUM_WAIT_OBJECTS handles. The high 32 bits are the process ID. */
+    wsprintfW(endpoint, L"\\pipe\\OLE_%08lx", (ULONG)(*oxid >> 32));
 }

